[al:新概念英语（三）]
[ar:MP3 同步字幕版（美音）]
[ti:A Day to Remember]
[by:更多学习内容，请到VeryCD.com搜索“露珠”]
[00:00.19]Lesson 33 | 第33课
[00:02.45]A day to remember | 难忘的一天
[00:08.98]What incident began the series of traffic accidents? | 是什么事件引发了这一连串交通事故？
[00:15.47]We have all experienced days when everything goes wrong. | 我们都经历过事事不顺心的日子。
[00:19.83]A day may begin well enough, but suddenly everything seems to get out of control. | 一天开始时可能还不错，但突然间似乎一切都失去了控制。
[00:25.98]What invariably happens is that a great number of things choose to go wrong at precisely the same moment. | 经常发生的是，众多事情偏偏在同一时刻出乱子。
[00:33.97]It is as if a single unimportant event set up a chain of reactions. | 仿佛一件无足轻重的小事引发了一连串连锁反应。
[00:39.93]Let us suppose that you are preparing a meal and keeping an eye on the baby at the same time. | 假设你正在做饭，同时又在照看孩子。
[00:47.23]The telephone rings and this marks the prelude to an unforeseen series of catastrophes. | 这时电话铃响了，它预示着一连串意想不到的灾难的序幕。
[00:55.44]While you are on the phone, the baby pulls the tablecloth off the table, | 你接电话时，孩子把桌布从桌子上扯了下来，
[01:01.12]smashing half your best crockery and cutting himself in the process. | 把最好的瓷器打碎了一半，同时也把自己划破了。
[01:06.49]You hang up hurriedly and attend to baby, crockery, etc. | 你急忙挂上电话，去照料孩子、收拾瓷器等。
[01:11.90]Meanwhile, the meal gets burnt. | 与此同时，饭烧糊了。
[01:14.62]As if this were not enough to reduce you to tears, your husband arrives, unexpectedly bringing three guests to dinner. | 好像这还不足以让你掉眼泪似的，你丈夫回来了，出人意料地带来3位客人吃饭。
[01:24.51]Things can go wrong on a big scale as a number of people recently discovered in Parramatta, a suburb of Sydney. | 就像悉尼郊区帕拉马塔的许多人新近发现的那样，乱子也能闹到很大规模。
[01:33.52]During the rush hour one evening two cars collided and both drivers began to argue. | 一天傍晚交通最拥挤的时候，两辆车相撞了，两位司机开始争吵起来。
[01:41.09]The woman immediately behind the two cars happened to be a learner. | 紧跟在这两辆车后面的一位司机碰巧是个新手。
[01:46.30]She suddenly got into a panic and stopped her car. | 她一下子惊慌失措，停下了车。
[01:50.97]This made the driver following her brake hard. | 这使得她后面的司机猛踩刹车。
[01:54.80]His wife was sitting beside him holding a large cake. | 他的妻子正坐在他身边，手里托着一块大蛋糕。
[01:59.08]As she was thrown forward, | 她被向前一甩，
[02:01.20]the cake went right through the windscreen and landed on the road. | 蛋糕正好从挡风玻璃上飞了出去，落到马路上。
[02:06.53]Seeing a cake flying through the air, | 看到一块蛋糕从天而降，
[02:09.42]a lorry driver who was drawing up alongside the car, pulled up all of a sudden. | 一辆正开过来与汽车并行的卡车司机猛地把车刹住了。
[02:16.17]The lorry was loaded with empty beer bottles and hundreds of them slid off the back of the vehicle and on to the road. | 卡车上装着空啤酒瓶，几百只瓶子从车尾滑落到马路上。
[02:25.47]This led to yet another angry argument. Meanwhile, the traffic piled up behind. | 这又引起一场愤怒的争吵。与此同时，后面的车辆排成了长龙。
[02:33.46]It took the police nearly an hour to get the traffic on the move again. | 警察花了将近一个小时才使交通重新畅通。
[02:38.96]In the meantime, the lorry driver had to sweep up hundreds of broken bottles. | 在此期间，卡车司机只得扫拢几百只碎瓶子。
[02:45.57]Only two stray dogs benefited from all this confusion, for they greedily devoured what was left of the cake. | 只有两只野狗从这场混乱中得到了好处——它们贪婪地吃掉了剩下的蛋糕。
[02:54.22]It was just one of those days! | 真是多灾多难的一天！
